/-
The shell module's paper: one declared base OS for every Kagamizo target.
Checked by checks.paper-shell (modules/repo/papers.nix).
-/
open Kagamizo.Paper

def paper : Paper := {
  module := "shell",
  title := "One declared shell for every target: a vertical column of apps, a generated settings app, and proofs of what ships where",
  abstract := "The shell module is Kagamizo's base operating system as a declaration. Targets (a NixOS image such as the Yoga C940 or the Raspberry Pi 5, a phone, or the browser) and apps (native programs, pages behind Access, or parts of the shell) are typed Nix options; one Rust program draws them as a single vertical column of cards with a settings app whose every row is generated from the repository; and Lean decides, before anything builds, that every app ships only where it can run.",
  authors := ["design", "upkeep"],
  date := "2026-11-07",
  sections := [
    { kind := .purpose,
      body := "The owner wants one coherent, stable base for everything they use, from a laptop to a browser to, later, a phone, with the simplicity of a console's home screen: a settings app, and every other app full screen in one vertical stack. Each device otherwise accretes its own launcher, its own settings and its own idea of which programs exist, and none of it is checked. This module replaces that with a declaration: what targets exist, which apps each one shows, and what every app is. The shell draws exactly that, the same way on every target, and the settings app shows only what the repository declares (targets, apps, accounts and teams), so a setting cannot exist that the repository does not know about. Nothing is emulated: a target either runs a program in its image or opens a page, and the proof says which.",
      cites := ["dolstra-thesis", "gamescope"] },
    { kind := .lifecycle,
      body := "Declared: shell.targets and shell.apps in shell.nix, each field typed (target kind nixos or web; app kind native, web or builtin; category from a closed list; targets only among the declared ones), so a misspelt target or kind fails evaluation. Built: the declarations are rendered to JSON (shell.rendered) and compiled into the Rust program at build time; packages.kagamizo-shell is the native build, packages.kagamizo-shell-web the WebAssembly build with its page. Deployed: a NixOS target imports _nixos.nix, which adds the package, sets KAGAMIZO_TARGET and replaces niri's config with the shell's session (modules/yoga-c940 does today); the web target is served by nginx on jingzo-host's loopback (_web-host.nix) and reached through the agents tunnel at os.jingzo.org behind Access, a route declared in hetzner.host.agents like every other host service. Changed: a new app or target is a one-attribute change of kind code, reviewed by design for the experience and upkeep for the build. Retired: deleting an app's attribute removes its card, its settings row and its proof obligations in the same commit.",
      cites := ["dolstra-thesis"] },
    { kind := .pipeline,
      body := "Every push runs checks.shell, which renders the targets, the apps and the PATH of every NixOS target's image (every binary of its environment.systemPackages) as Lean facts and decides shell_sound by the kernel: if an app is declared on a target it cannot run on, or a native app's command is missing from the image, the build fails before any image is made. The native package runs the model's unit tests (cargo test) as part of its build; the web package builds the library for wasm32-unknown-unknown with a rust-overlay toolchain and binds it with the wasm-bindgen CLI pinned to the lockfile's version. checks.paper-shell holds this paper to the module, and statix, deadnix, formatting and languages lint its files. The Yoga's image includes the shell through its nixosConfiguration, so building that image builds the shell.",
      cites := ["lean4"] },
    { kind := .agents,
      body := "design owns the experience: the column, the settings layout, the palette, and the rule that every screen works at a phone's width and a desktop's, which it reviews from screenshots of the web build at 390 and 1280 pixels. upkeep owns the build: the Rust toolchain and egui pins, the wasm-bindgen version that must match the lockfile, and the per-target images. Neither team holds any grant over an account here: the shell reads only what the repository declares, opens pages the owner already reaches through Access, and starts programs already in the image. A new target (the Raspberry Pi, a GrapheneOS phone's browser) is proposed by design and built by upkeep, and the owner sees it as one more row under System.",
      cites := ["gamescope"] },
    { kind := .tools,
      body := "kagamizo-shell: the shell itself. Natively it opens one full-screen window (app id kagamizo-shell) and reads its target from KAGAMIZO_TARGET; enter on a native app moves niri to its empty bottom workspace (niri msg --json workspaces, then focus-workspace) and spawns the command there, so each app lands below the last; enter on a web app opens it in a new browser window. In the browser it draws on a canvas it creates, and a web app opens in a new tab. Keys: up and down move the column, enter opens, escape or backspace goes back, left and right move between the settings sections and their rows; on a touch screen a tap does the same. shell-page: writes the web target's index.html. niri.kdl: the session's compositor config, with Mod+Escape for home and Mod+Up and Mod+Down between apps.",
      cites := ["niri", "egui"] },
    { kind := .data,
      body := "The only data is the repository's own declarations, rendered at build time into shell.json and compiled into the binary: the targets (name, kind, host and why), the apps (name, category, kind, command or url, targets), and the settings sections generated from them plus accounts.held (provider and plan) and agents.charters (each team's goal). Nothing is read at run time but KAGAMIZO_TARGET and, under niri, the compositor's list of workspaces. Nothing is written. No secret, no personal data and no account value enters the shell: the accounts section shows which accounts are declared and on which plan, never a credential, because the declarations hold none. The repository is public, and so is everything the shell can show.",
      cites := ["dolstra-thesis"] },
    { kind := .design,
      body := "Three layers. Lean (_lean/Shell.lean) states what a sound shell is: unique app and target ids, every app on at least one declared target, no native app on the web target, and every native app's command on the PATH of each image it ships in. The home is a library, in the way a game console's is: every module of the repository registers itself in its own library.nix, either as part of the base (shell.base, a section of Settings: system, devices, accounts, identity, governance, infrastructure, repository) or as apps (shell.apps, a card on a shelf, one shelf per category); a module with no screen of its own registers a card of kind module, whose page is generated from its declaration and its paper (title, abstract, checks, packages, teams). Rust (_shell) splits a pure model from its drawing: model.rs holds the declared apps for one target, the focus, and a key and tap state machine that returns an effect (none, run a command, open a url), tested without a screen; lib.rs draws it with egui, an immediate-mode toolkit, so the screen is a function of the model each frame and there is no widget state to drift. The same library compiles natively (glow on Wayland) and to WebAssembly (eframe's web runner), so the browser target is the same program, not a port. Nix types the declarations, builds both outputs, and joins Lean to the facts. niri was chosen because its workspaces already stack vertically, so 'every app full screen in one vertical stack' is the compositor's own model, not something the shell fakes.",
      cites := ["egui", "imgui", "niri", "wasm"] },
    { kind := .guarantees,
      body := "checks.shell decides, by the kernel, shell_sound for the declared targets, apps and image PATHs: ids are unique, every app ships on a declared target, a browser or phone target (the web target, the Pixel) holds only web and built-in apps, and every native app's command is a binary in each image that ships it. The general theorem web_never_native proves, for any app that passes appSound, that on any browser or phone target the app is not native: a browser or a phone is never asked to run one of our programs, whatever is declared later. Together these mean a card on any target opens something that exists there: a program in that image, a page, or the shell's own settings. The library theorem covered_visible proves that when library_covered holds, which checks.shell decides for the repository's modules, every module is either part of the base (a row of Settings) or registers an app (a card): no module of the repository is invisible, and a new one fails the build until it says which it is. The model's unit tests add the run-time half: a target shows only its own apps, settings first; the column stops at its ends; taps do what keys do; an empty section is never entered.",
      cites := ["lean4"] },
    { kind := .performance,
      body := "No perf target is declared for this module yet. What is measured today: the web build is 2.53 MB of WebAssembly (release, opt-level s) plus 74 kB of wasm-bindgen's generated module, and it draws its first frame in under three seconds in headless Chromium on a software renderer. The native shell idles at no redraw unless input arrives, because egui repaints on events. What moves these: the font atlas and egui's default fonts dominate the wasm size; wasm-opt and dropping unused egui features would cut it, and a declared perf target (first frame and transfer size of the web build, measured in CI's Chromium) is the next step so the number can only fall.",
      cites := ["wasm", "egui"] },
    { kind := .priorWork,
      body := "Valve's Steam Deck runs Game Mode on gamescope, a compositor that shows one app at a time over a launcher, and the Switch's home is a single row of software cards with a settings app beside it; the shell takes their one-app-in-front model but declares it. niri, after PaperWM, tiles windows in columns on workspaces that stack vertically, which gives the vertical app stack directly. egui implements the immediate-mode GUI idea Muratori described, which keeps the shell's screen a pure function of its model. WebAssembly, as Haas et al. designed it, lets the same Rust run in the browser at near-native speed. Nix's purely functional deployment (Dolstra) makes each target an image built from the declaration, and Lean 4 decides the declaration's soundness as a kernel-checked proof.",
      cites := ["gamescope", "niri", "paperwm", "imgui", "wasm", "dolstra-thesis", "lean4"] },
    { kind := .gaps,
      body := "os.jingzo.org is declared (tunnel route, Access application, DNS record) but answers only once the Cloudflare layer is applied, which the owner approves, and the host has switched to a configuration that serves it; until then the browser build is checked by screenshots in a session. The web page carries a one-line module import that starts wasm-bindgen's loader, because a browser loads WebAssembly only from a module script; it is emitted by shell-page (Rust), and goes when browsers load a wasm module directly. The Yoga's session has not yet been booted with the shell: the container has no GPU, so the native build is verified by its build and tests, not by sight. There is no gamepad input yet (gilrs natively, the Gamepad API in the browser), so Steam is not yet declared as an app, and the Raspberry Pi 5 and a GrapheneOS phone's browser are not yet targets. No perf target is declared. Each is a backlog item for design and upkeep.",
      cites := ["gamescope"] }
  ],
  citations := [
    { key := "dolstra-thesis", title := "The Purely Functional Software Deployment Model", authors := ["Eelco Dolstra"], year := 2006,
      source := .url "https://edolstra.github.io/pubs/phd-thesis.pdf" },
    { key := "lean4", title := "The Lean 4 Theorem Prover and Programming Language", authors := ["Leonardo de Moura", "Sebastian Ullrich"], year := 2021,
      source := .doi "10.1007/978-3-030-79876-5_37" },
    { key := "wasm", title := "Bringing the Web up to Speed with WebAssembly", authors := ["Andreas Haas", "Andreas Rossberg", "Derek L. Schuff", "Ben L. Titzer", "Michael Holman", "Dan Gohman", "Luke Wagner", "Alon Zakai", "JF Bastien"], year := 2017,
      source := .doi "10.1145/3062341.3062363" },
    { key := "imgui", title := "Immediate-Mode Graphical User Interfaces", authors := ["Casey Muratori"], year := 2005,
      source := .url "https://caseymuratori.com/blog_0001" },
    { key := "egui", title := "egui: an easy-to-use immediate mode GUI in Rust", authors := ["Emil Ernerfeldt"], year := 2025,
      source := .url "https://github.com/emilk/egui" },
    { key := "niri", title := "niri: a scrollable-tiling Wayland compositor", authors := ["Ivan Molodetskikh"], year := 2025,
      source := .url "https://github.com/YaLTeR/niri" },
    { key := "paperwm", title := "PaperWM: tiled scrollable window management for GNOME Shell", authors := ["PaperWM contributors"], year := 2024,
      source := .url "https://github.com/paperwm/PaperWM" },
    { key := "gamescope", title := "gamescope: the SteamOS session compositing window manager", authors := ["Valve Software"], year := 2025,
      source := .url "https://github.com/ValveSoftware/gamescope" }
  ],
  theorems := ["web_never_native", "covered_visible"],
  perf := [],
  checks := ["shell", "paper-shell"],
  packages := ["kagamizo-shell", "kagamizo-shell-web"],
  agents := ["design", "upkeep"]
}
